.SH "EXIT STATUS"
Exit status: 0 = done; 2 = an error, a usage error included; 75 = the catalog was busy, run it again. A command with codes of its own (audit, host check, config check, db fsck, volume verify, cartridge erase, the commands that write a tape, the drive commands) names them in its own \-\-help, and the CLI reference has every code in one table.
.TP
\fB0\fR
done: the command did what it was asked, or what it checks is clean (every command)
.TP
\fB1\fR
done, with warnings: the work stands and something deserves a look (`audit` (warnings only); `host check` (something tripped); `db fsck` (problems found, repaired or not); `collection sync`, `status`, `plan` and `run` (an entry outside any unit, a folder refused, a unit marked missing or refused); `drive poll` (a TapeAlert or an unrecovered error); `drive cleaned` (the cleaning cartridge at or past its rated life); `unit check\-integrity` (a damaged or missing file))
.TP
\fB2\fR
an error, a usage error included: nothing was decided (every command, except the meanings below)
.TP
\fB2\fR
a verdict, not an error: at least one violation (`audit`); the medium proven bad and the volume quarantined (`volume verify`); the configuration invalid (`config check`); the database's integrity broken (`db fsck`); the catalog unreadable (`status`) (`audit`, `volume verify`, `config check`, `db fsck`, `status`)
.TP
\fB2\fR
refused, the medium untouched (the commands that write a tape (`volume write`, `volume resume`, `volume rewrite`, `volume compact\-write`, `volume compact`, `collection run`, `quick\-archive`) and `cartridge erase`)
.TP
\fB3\fR
inconclusive: no verdict about the medium, which is untouched; every error of `volume verify`, a usage error after `verify` included (`volume verify`; the commands that write a tape (sealed, the confirm inconclusive); `cartridge erase` (erased, the read\-back inconclusive))
.TP
\fB4\fR
interrupted: `volume resume` continues the session (`cartridge erase`: the erase failed part\-way, run it again) (the commands that write a tape, `cartridge erase`)
.TP
\fB5\fR
aborted: the session cannot resume, write the label again (the commands that write a tape)
.TP
\fB6\fR
the medium proven bad: the volume is quarantined (`cartridge erase`: the read\-back contradicts the erase) (the commands that write a tape, `cartridge erase`)
.TP
\fB70\fR
no verdict: the audit stopped on an error, a usage error after `audit` included (`audit`)
.TP
\fB75\fR
busy: another tapectl process held the catalog (or the drive) too long; nothing changed, run it again (every command that opens the catalog, except `volume verify` (3); `drive poll` and `drive eject` when a command holds the drive)
